-- title:
--   The function field of X₁(M) over ℚ̄ is a curve
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a natural number with `NeZero M`, i.e. $M \neq 0$. Write $F$ for [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar M`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182): by definition this is `laurentBaseChange (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (x1FunctionField M)`, namely the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise embedding $\mathbb{Q}((q)) \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$, of the $q$-expansion function field `x1FunctionFieldC ℚ M` $\subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$ attached to $X_1(M)$. The assertion is `IsCurveOver (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) F`, which packages three statements. First, principal divisors exist and have degree zero: for every $f \in F$ with $f \neq 0$ there is a divisor $D$ on $F/\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $D(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f)$ at every place $v$ and $\deg D = 0$, where a place is a valuation subring of $F$ containing $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, distinct from $F$ itself, and a principal ideal ring. Second, every such place has residue field finite-dimensional over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Third, the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{F/\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}$ is free over $F$ of rank one.
--
--   This records that the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(M)$ is a one-variable algebraic function field in the sense of the project's `IsCurveOver` axioms, so that the divisor, Riemann–Roch and differential machinery for curves applies to it. It is used throughout the study of $X_1(M)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, in particular in the comparison of regular differentials with weight-two cusp forms and in the analysis of reductions and Weil pairings on the Jacobian.
